From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Tue, 17 May 1994 01:27:37 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-207] Is AI possible?

In Message #94-5-199   Tim Goodwin <goodwin@snug.demon.co.uk> writes:
 
>I'll never believe a machine is intelligent until it does the equivalent 
>of watching another machine slipping on a banana skin and laughing at the
>resulting pain and loss of dignity.......
 
 [ Who said that humor is laughing at somebody who fell, and satire - at
somebody who pushed ? ]
 
 It's not hard to program a debugger or a test program to play 'giggle.wav' 
or print "Ha-ha-ha" on the screen when a program running under it divides
by zero, etc.
 
  All definitions of humor seem to describe a set of:
  
  1) situations, usually social:
  
  (like: unusual events, sudden changes in context, something going wrong,
situations that you may think of but can not create due to environmental
limitations, e.g. you want to release libido and aggression in practice,
but instead tell a story about some manager's wife getting raped by a
gorilla.)
   and
 2) Reactions, usually specific to human physiology and inter-personal
 communications.
 
   Computers can detect analogies of most 'humorous' situations, and
can easily produce similar or any other reactions.
   If programmed to mimic such reactions, the computer may still not
understand what it is doing, but since humans usually have no interest,
let alone undertanding, of the nature of their humor, this will hardly
represent a difference.
 
   Also, things like dignity, sexuality, aggression, limited pain tolerance
and other building blocks of humorous situations seem to be very specific
for human reproduction techniques, physiological design and structure of
primitive semi-competitive, semi-cooperative tribes.
   I do not see why an entity coming from a drastically different background 
should have any features like this.
 
   Also, though I still have some groups of neurons that recognize the
banana-skin-slip situations at make me giggle in a company supposedly
expecting it, consciously I consider people's connections of such slips
with their perception of 'lost dignity' as a typical part of an atavistic
mind-set, rather than an indicator of great Intelligence.
 
   
