> Phil and Hanno co-comment about animal intelligence and behavior.
 
  I think I mentioned here, at the time _Jurassic_Park_ came out, that all
_Velociraptor_ behavior portrayed is taken from documented behavior of
contemporary carnivores, perhaps with the exception of the very systematic
search pattern in the Park kitchen.
 
  I believe that SF author James H. Schmitz once wrote a short story -- one
of the early Tezley Amberdon ones -- that featured a hunting reserve jointly
operated by humans and a species of intelligent carnivores, in which each was
the prey for the other.  It took a little negociation about what technology
was allowed, to keep the kill/be-killed probability percentages at 50/50
(the humans needed some weapons, the carnivores didn't), but other than that,
everybody thought it was just fine...

  I have never seen a report of bears hunting in packs, and would be extremely
interested in a reference.  However, contemporary brown bears (of which
grizzly and Kodiak are the best-known subspecies) are at times social, to the
extent of congregating at places where there is a lot of food, such as mountain
meadows where all the berries have just bloomed, or garbage dumps.

